Cisco OpenH264 (BSD 2-Clause for the source; binary licence for Cisco's
builds). Loaded at run time, never shipped, linked or downloaded.

  Source:   https://github.com/cisco/openh264
  Used by:  ghostframe-lib/src/encoder/h264/openh264.rs and openh264_sys.rs,
            which `dlopen` libopenh264 (libopenh264.so.8 for 2.6.x,
            libopenh264.so.7 for 2.4.x and 2.5.x, then libopenh264.so, or the
            absolute path in GHOSTFRAME_H264_OPENH264_LIB) and call its
            public C API. The structure layouts in openh264_sys.rs are
            written from the public headers (codec_api.h, codec_app_def.h),
            which are not copied here.

  OpenH264 Video Codec provided by Cisco Systems, Inc.

  Ghostframe contains no OpenH264 code or binary. A machine that runs the
  server with the library installed uses it for software H.264 encoding when
  no hardware encoder serves the stream, or when GHOSTFRAME_H264_BACKEND is
  openh264. Whether that use is covered by a patent licence is the
  operator's to decide:

    * Cisco's patent grant covers only the binary Cisco itself builds and
      distributes (https://www.openh264.org/BINARY_LICENSE.txt), under
      conditions including that the user can turn the codec off and that the
      attribution above is shown. Ghostframe meets the second by logging the
      sentence when a session opens ("openh264 session open") and the first
      with GHOSTFRAME_H264_BACKEND=vaapi (libva-direct only), which never
      loads the library.
    * A library built by a distribution from the source (Debian and Ubuntu's
      libopenh264-7, Fedora's own build) carries no such grant. The operator
      who installs one, or who pulls Cisco's binary from Cisco's repository,
      is the licensee; this project fetches nothing.
